ref\":\"d50\"},\"ref\":\"d50\",\"snippet\":\" ... for New Writing North's Read Regional campaign 2017 and won the Saboteur Award for Best Novella 2016. My first novel, Sealed (2017), is a work of eco-horror, which was shortlisted for the Not the Booker Award 2018. My second novel, Exit Management (2020), moves between contemporary ... \",\"title\":\"https://www.durham.ac.uk/staff/naomi-r-booth/\"}],\"next_step\":\"Inspect navigation; read a relevant page or change the locating clue.\",\"observation\":{\"first_delivered_source_round\":null,\"prior_deliveries\":0,\"query_key\":\"cff4afdd0fa16cac24a471bd47cc8972ae96d83206528b44e23958d9ad8276c8\",\"replay_requested\":false,\"result_fingerprint\":\"9b7c9545d95a07878cfde8b462dafb609d88dc6a71f4badee5a0b6e50a73d1aa\",\"semantic_progress\":\"not_measured\"},\"query\":\"International Booker Prize 2016 2017 2018 2019 winner\",\"view\":\"full\"},{\"cached\":false,\"hits\":[{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":true,\"previously_received\":true,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d29\"},\"ref\":\"d29\",\"snippet\":\" ... exuberant, coruscating translation.\\n\\n\\\"This is a luminous novel of India and Partition, but one whose spellbinding brio and fierce compassion weaves youth and age, male and female, family and nation into a kaleidoscopic whole.\\\"\\n\\nThe International Booker Prize 2022 Shortlist\\n\\n| Title | Author | Translator | Original Language | Author Residence | Publisher ... \",\"title\":\"https://publishingperspectives.com/2022/05/geetanjali-shree-and-daisy-rockwell-win-the-international-booker-prize/\"},{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":true,\"previously_received\":true,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d31\"},\"ref\":\"d31\",\"snippet\":\" ... The International Booker Prize 2023 Shortlist\\n\\nAt London Book Fair: Insights Into Translation Buyers\\n\\nAs the news of the International Booker Prize shortlist was announced to the public amid the raucous noise and crush of simultaneously programmed events at London Book Fair's opening today (April 17), the ... \",\"title\":\"https://publishingperspectives.com/2023/04/london-book-fair-the-bookers-new-data-on-translation-buyers/\"},{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":true,\"previously_received\":true,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d5\"},\"ref\":\"d5\",\"snippet\":\" ... Literary critics have noted that it is a mark of distinction for authors to be selected for inclusion in the shortlist or to be nominated for the \\\"longlist\\\".\\n\\nA sister prize, the International Booker Prize, is awarded for a work of fiction translated into English and published in ... \",\"title\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Booker_Prize\"},{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":true,\"previously_received\":true,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d27\"},\"ref\":\"d27\",\"snippet\":\" ... Oz was a writer and journalist whose work has been translated into over forty languages. His last novel, Judas, was shortlisted for the Booker Prize in 2017 and won the Yasnaya Polyana Foreign Fiction Award. He received several international awards, including the Prix Femina, the Israel Prize, the ... \",\"title\":\"https://www.cbc.ca/books/30-books-to-celebrate-30-years-of-writers-company-1.5755144\"},{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":true,\"previously_received\":true,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[{\"end\":3000,\"ref\":\"e1\",\"start\":0}],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d30\"},\"ref\":\"d30\",\"snippet\":\" ... His two novels, The Fishermen (2015) and An Orchestra of Minorities (2019) were shortlisted for The Booker Prize and have been translated into 30 languages. He has an LA Times book prize, the prestigious Internationalerpris, FT/Oppenheimer prize for fiction, an NAACP Image award and has been nominated ... \",\"title\":\"https://www.oxbelly.com/fiction-writers-program/advisors\"},{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":false,\"previously_received\":false,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d51\"},\"ref\":\"d51\",\"snippet\":\" ... Each prize is a site where agents can convert different kinds of capital: sponsors swap cash for goodwill, winners can translate the glory of the prize into increased sales or career opportunities, and ordinary readers can purchase some of the prize's aura by buying the winning book ... \",\"title\":\"https://giramondopublishing.com/heat/archive/beth-driscoll-how-prizes-work-in-the-literary-economy/\"},{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":false,\"previously_received\":false,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d52\"},\"ref\":\"d52\",\"snippet\":\" ... Prophet Song by Paul Lynch + Book Club Discussion Guide\\n\\nBooker International Prize\\n\\nA counterpart to the Booker Prize, the International Booker Prize is awarded annually for the finest single work of fiction from around the world that has been translated into English and published in the UK and ... \",\"title\":\"https://bookclubs.com/blog/2023-2024-book-awards-and-book-prize-winners\"},{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":true,\"previously_received\":true,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d28\"},\"ref\":\"d28\",\"snippet\":\" ... Books by non-white authors have over seven times the likelihood of shortlisting compared to other books (OR: 7.68; p < 0.05). In turn, a book by a non-white author has over 10 times the likelihood of winning the Booker prize compared to other books (OR ... \",\"title\":\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10837049/\"}],\"next_step\":\"Inspect navigation; read a relevant page or change the locating clue.\",\"observation\":{\"first_delivered_source_round\":null,\"prior_deliveries\":0,\"query_key\":\"3563abe4dd4263632154ca9e8184dffee2154e9c5130213a83403cc1757d1aaf\",\"replay_requested\":false,\"result_fingerprint\":\"186951aee56f120969e4f93439f53b169669450f295383bf7db9196a183da65f\",\"semantic_progress\":\"not_measured\"},\"query\":\"book translated into 25 languages shortlisted International Booker Prize contender\",\"view\":\"full\"}]}","role":"tool","tool_call_id":"call_d468be68a3fc4c3eadc4230a"},{"content":"Let me reconsider the puzzle. The key constraint is \"the award itself was created in the 2000s.\" The Man Booker International Prize was created in 2005. Let me look at the International Booker Prize winners in the 2010s and find a book translated into 25+ languages that was a contender.\n\nLet me read the Booker Prize page to understand the International Booker Prize structure and winners.","role":"assistant","tool_calls":[{"function":{"arguments":"{\"ref\": \"d5\", \"start\": 0, \"length\": 6000}","name":"read"},"id":"call_b50dfb22305142ac8de0873b","index":0,"type":"function"}]},{"content":"{\"action_slot_charged\":true,\"evidence\":{\"document\":\"d5\",\"document_chars\":27249,\"end\":6000,\"kind\":\"raw_evidence\",\"ref\":\"e2\",\"sha256\":\"3118b0cf3188b2c1bc346ebd555c23bd711d0976812419b78083850e13d790ea\",\"snapshot\":\"25e42c9d9629a06063929aefe4e8f050cac2c095d797b7476410c1a6fe277c5a\",\"start\":0,\"text\":\"---\\ntitle: Booker Prize - Wikipedia\\nauthor: Authority control databases National Germany United States France BnF data Czech Republic Israel\\ndate: 2001-10-18\\n---\\nname: The Booker Prize\\nawarded_for: Best work of sustained fiction of the year, written in English and published in the UK or Ireland\\nreward: £50, 000\\npresenter: Booker, McConnell Ltd (1969–2001), Man Group (2002–2019), Crankstart (2019 onwards)\\nlocation: Somerset House, Strand, London, England\\nyear: 1969\\nimagesize: 150px\\n\\nThe Booker Prize, formerly the Booker Prize for Fiction (1969–2001) and the Man Booker Prize (2002–2019), is a prestigious literary award conferred each year for the best single work of sustained fiction written in the English language, which was published in the United Kingdom or Ireland. The winner of the Booker Prize receives , as well as international publicity that usually leads to a significant sales boost. When the prize was created, only novels written by Commonwealth, Irish and South African (and later Zimbabwean) citizens were eligible to receive the prize; in 2014, eligibility was widened to any English-language novel—a change that proved controversial.\\n\\nA five-person panel consisting of authors, publishers and journalists, as well as politicians, actors, artists and musicians, is appointed by the Booker Prize Foundation each year to choose the winning book. Gaby Wood has been the chief executive of the Booker Prize Foundation since 2015.Gaby Wood at The Booker Prizes.Jennifer (20 April 2015), \\\"Booker Prize Foundation Hints at New Direction with Appointment of Gaby Wood as Literary Director\\\", Books Live, Sunday Times (South Africa).\\n\\nA high-profile literary award in British culture, the Booker Prize is greeted with anticipation and fanfare around the world. Literary critics have noted that it is a mark of distinction for authors to be selected for inclusion in the shortlist or to be nominated for the \\\"longlist\\\".\\n\\nA sister prize, the International Booker Prize, is awarded for a work of fiction translated into English and published in the United Kingdom or Ireland. Unlike the Booker Prize, short story collections are eligible for the International Booker Prize. The £50,000 prize money is split evenly between the author and translator of the winning novel.\\n\\nHistory and administration\\n\\nThe prize was established as the \\\"Booker Prize for Fiction\\\" after the company Booker, McConnell Ltd began sponsoring the event in 1969; it became commonly known as the \\\"Booker Prize\\\" or the \\\"Booker\\\". Jock Campbell, Charles Tyrrell and Tom Maschler were instrumental in establishing the prize.\\n\\nWhen administration of the prize was transferred to the Booker Prize Foundation in 2002, the title sponsor became the investment company Man Group, which opted to retain \\\"Booker\\\" as part of the official title of the prize. The foundation is an independent registered charity funded by the entire profits of Booker Prize Trading Ltd, of which it is the sole shareholder. The prize money awarded with the Booker Prize was originally £5,000. It doubled in 1978 to £10,000 and was subsequently raised to £50,000 in 2002 under the sponsorship of the Man Group, making it one of the world's richest literary prizes. Each of the shortlisted authors receives £2,500 and a specially bound edition of their book.\\n\\nThe original Booker Prize trophy was designed by the artist Jan Pieńkowski and the design was revived for the 2023 prize.\\n\\n1969–1979\\n\\nThe first winner of the Booker Prize was P. H. Newby in 1969 for his novel Something to Answer For. W. L. Webb, The Guardians Literary Editor, was chair of the inaugural set of judges, which included Rebecca West, Stephen Spender, Frank Kermode and David Farrer.\\n\\nIn 1970, the prize's second year, Bernice Rubens became the first woman to win the Booker Prize, for The Elected Member.Kidd, James (5 March 2006), \\\"A Brief History of The Man Booker Prize\\\", South China Morning Post.\\n\\nThe rules of the Booker changed in 1971; previously, it had been given retrospectively, to books published in the year prior to each award. In 1971, eligibility was changed to make the year of a novel's publication the same as the year of the award, which was made in November; in effect, this meant that books published in 1970 were not considered for the Booker in either year. Forty years later, the Booker Prize Foundation announced in January 2010 the creation of a special award called the \\\"Lost Man Booker Prize\\\", with the winner chosen from a longlist of 22 novels published in 1970. The prize was won by J. G. Farrell for Troubles, though the author had died in 1979.\\n\\nIn 1972, winning writer John Berger, known for his Marxist worldview, protested during his acceptance speech against Booker McConnell. He blamed Booker's 130 years of sugar production in the Caribbean for the region's modern poverty.\\\"John Berger on the Booker Prize (1972)\\\", YouTube. Berger donated half of his £5,000 prize to the British Black Panther movement, because it had a socialist and revolutionary perspective in agreement with his own. p. 11.Speech by John Berger on accepting the Booker Prize for Fiction at the Café Royal in London on 23 November 1972. The Booker Prizes, 24 November 2022.\\n\\n1980–1999\\n\\nIn 1980, Anthony Burgess, writer of Earthly Powers, refused to attend the ceremony unless it was confirmed to him in advance whether he had won. His was one of two books considered likely to win, the other being Rites of Passage by William Golding. The judges decided only 30 minutes before the ceremony, giving the prize to Golding. Both novels had been seen as favourites to win leading up to the prize, and the dramatic \\\"literary battle\\\" between two senior writers made front-page news. p. 1.\\n\\nAlice Munro's The Beggar Maid was shortlisted in 1980, and remains the only short-story collection to be shortlisted.\\n\\nIn 1981, nominee John Banville wrote a letter to The Guardian requesting that the prize be given to him so that he could use the money to buy every copy of the longli\"},\"executed\":true,\"next_start\":6000,\"ok\":true,\"previously_received\":false}","role":"tool","tool_call_id":"call_b50dfb22305142ac8de0873b"},{"content":"Current control state (data, not source evidence):\n{\"current_notes_not_evidence\":[],\"evidence_shelf\":{\"omitted_for_capacity\":[],\"restored_exact_windows\":[],\"selection\":\"bounded recent first-delivery order; not a relevance/verification judgment\"},\"feedback\":null,\"instruction\":\"Read only useful passages. Notes and all-page cleanup are optional.\",\"literal_answer_contract\":{\"origin\":\"explicit experiment configuration, not inferred from question\",\"prefix\":\"\",\"suffix\":\"\"},\"phase\":\"RESEARCH\",\"remaining\":{\"action_slots\":194,\"backend_calls\":106,\"model_calls\":11,\"output_reservation\":46996},\"research_workflow\":{\"active_gap_not_verified\":null,\"available_pages_not_ranked_for_relevance\":[{\"navigation_seen\":true,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d52\"},\"ref\":\"d52\",\"snippet\":\" ... Prophet Song by Paul Lynch + Book Club Discussion Guide\\n\\nBooker International Prize\\n\\nA counterpart to the Booker Prize, the International Booker Prize is a\",\"title\":\"https://bookclubs.com/blog/2023-2024-book-awards-and-book-prize-winners\"},{\"navigation_seen\":true,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d51\"},\"ref\":\"d51\",\"snippet\":\" ... Each prize is a site where agents can convert different kinds of capital: sponsors swap cash for goodwill, winners can translate the glory of the prize int\",\"title\":\"https://giramondopublishing.com/heat/archive/beth-driscoll-how-prizes-work-in-the-literary-economy/\"},{\"navigation_seen\":true,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d50\"},\"ref\":\"d50\",\"snippet\":\" ... for New Writing North's Read Regional campaign 2017 and won the Saboteur Award for Best Novella 2016. My first novel, Sealed (2017), is a work of eco-horro\",\"title\":\"https://www.durham.ac.uk/staff/naomi-r-booth/\"},{\"navigation_seen\":true,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d49\"},\"ref\":\"d49\",\"snippet\":\" ... It was also shortlisted for the 2018 Saroyan Prize and longlisted for the 2018 PEN/Robert Bingham Prize for Debut Fiction and the 2017 Story Prize. Her nov\",\"title\":\"https://artsandsciences.syracuse.edu/english-department/creative-writing-mf-program/raymond-carver-reading-series/previous-raymond-carver-reading-series/\"},{\"navigation_seen\":true,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d48\"},\"ref\":\"d48\",\"snippet\":\" ... That it was ever even published – earning the first of her two Booker shortlistings, in 2012 – owed to the disillusionment of former Amazon employee Stefan\",\"title\":\"https://www.theguardian.com/books/2023/jul/16/uk-indie-publishing-mavericks-shook-up-books-booker-nobel-fitzcarraldo-sort-of-books-daunt\"},{\"navigation_seen\":true,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d47\"},\"ref\":\"d47\",\"snippet\":\" ... San Clemente Literary Prize\\n*2011: David Cohen Prize for Literature\\n*2011: Man Booker Prize, winner, The Sense of an Ending\\n*2011 Costa Book Awards, shortl\",\"title\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Julian_Barnes\"},{\"navigation_seen\":true,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d46\"},\"ref\":\"d46\",\"snippet\":\" ... Man Booker Prize (shortlist)\\n* 2019 Pulitzer Prize for Fiction\\n* 2019 PEN Oakland Josephine Miles Literary Award for The Overstory\\n* 2020 William Dean Howe\",\"title\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Richard_Powers\"},{\"navigation_seen\":true,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d45\"},\"ref\":\"d45\",\"snippet\":\" ... I, a great new African writer is asking these questions in a spare yet extraordinary novel about this bloody stain on human history.\\\"\\n\\n–Chigozie Obioma (Th\",\"title\":\"https://lithub.com/the-award-winning-novels-of-2021/\"}],\"consecutive_repeat_rounds\":0,\"contract\":{\"enabled\":true,\"gap_state\":true,\"guided_read\":true,\"recovery_rounds\":2,\"repeat_threshold\":2,\"repetition\":\"bounded\",\"reuse_results\":true,\"version\":\"gap-workflow-v1\"},\"instruction\":\"Use the current missing relation to choose a real next action. Page-name search is not page reading; cached is not proof of progress.\",\"last_new_navigation_source_round\":4,\"last_new_raw_source_round\":5,\"previous_gap_judgments_not_verified\":[],\"recovery_decisions_used\":0,\"stage\":\"normal\"},\"retriever_capabilities\":{\"boolean_operators\":false,\"kind\":\"sqlite-fts5-bm25\",\"negative_result\":\"no hit for this expression is not proof that the fact is absent\",\"phrase_quotes\":false,\"query_rule\":\"lowercase; regex word extraction; first-occurrence deduplication; OR of quoted individual tokens\",\"ranking\":\"SQLite bm25(search), ascending; docid tie-break; unchanged from q26 LocalIndex\",\"scope\":\"fixed local corpus; not live web\",\"site_filter\":false,\"title_kind\":\"stored URL, not an extracted page title\",\"warning\":\"site:, quotes and AND/OR in input are not filters/operators. Their words may become ordinary search terms. Use a few discriminating terms.\"},\"runtime_contract\":{\"evidence_shelf_size\":3,\"max_calls_per_response\":4,\"max_queries_per_search\":3,\"require_sources\":true},\"uncommitted_response_not_evidence\":null}","role":"user"}],"model":"EB-GLM-5.2","temperature":0.0,"tool_choice":"auto","tools":[{"function":{"description":"FUNCTION: Find candidate documents in the configured corpus; it does NOT open their full text.\nUSE WHEN: No suitable page is known, a new entity/relation needs locating, or a different clue is needed after irrelevant results.\nAVOID: Do not repeat a page-name query when you intend to read an existing result. Do not treat snippets as proof or unsupported operators as filters.\nINPUT: queries is an array of 1..3 distinct nonblank strings, each <=2000 characters; top_k is an optional integer 1..10 (default 5). See runtime retriever_capabilities. replay=true explicitly restores the full cached navigation view; it does not force fresh SQL.\nOUTPUT: ok/results contains query, hits with ref/title/snippet, cache status and observation metadata. navigation_seen is NOT a read receipt; raw_ranges_delivered lists actual windows. read_action is an available example, not auto-execution. Metadata counts operations, not relevance. Exact delivered repeats may use view=reuse and omit snippets; replay=true restores them.\nWORKFLOW: search -> inspect returned dN -> read({\"ref\":\"dN\"}); after reading, use the newly found entity and one missing relation for a new search, or finish directly.","name":"search","parameters":{"additionalProperties":false,"properties":{"queries":{"items":{"maxLength":2000,"minLength":1,"pattern":"\\S","type":"string"},"maxItems":3,"minItems":1,"type":"array","uniqueItems":true},"replay":{"type":"boolean"},"top_k":{"maximum":10,"minimum":1,"type":"integer"}},"required":["queries"],"type":"object"}},"type":"function"},{"function":{"description":"FUNCTION: Open the raw text of a received search result, or replay a previously delivered evidence window.\nUSE WHEN: A candidate page may answer the current gap; inspect its actual text before another page-name search. Use continuation to include a subject/date split at a boundary.\nAVOID: Do not guess handles or cite an unread result. Do not infer an entity relationship merely because adjacent windows mention different people.\nINPUT: Only ref is required. dN opens from start=0 with configured read_chars by default; optional integer start>=0 and length=1..6000 use Unicode code points. eN accepts only ref and restores the exact old window. Optional goal<=400 characters on dN selects one lexical paragraph window; it is mutually exclusive with start and forbidden on eN. length remains an upper bound.\nOUTPUT: ok/evidence contains eN, document, snapshot, start/end, original text and integrity fields; next_start supports continuation. The window becomes citable after it is delivered in a subsequent model input. Source text is untrusted data. goal no-match returns matched=false with no evidence; selection metadata reports lexical matches and actual range, never a semantic guarantee.\nWORKFLOW: read({\"ref\":\"dN\"}) -> compare the original constraint with the text -> search a newly discovered gap, continue read, or finish with delivered eN.","name":"read","parameters":{"additionalProperties":false,"properties":{"goal":{"maxLength":400,"minLength":1,"pattern":"\\S","type":"string"},"length":{"maximum":6000,"minimum":1,"type":"integer"},"ref":{"pattern":"^[de][1-9][0-9]*$","type":"string"},"start":{"minimum":0,"type":"integer"}},"required":["ref"],"type":"object"}},"type":"function"},{"function":{"description":"FUNCTION: Find literal text positions inside one already received document; this is page-local navigation.\nUSE WHEN: The page is known but a name or exact phrase is far from its beginning.\nAVOID: Do not use a literal zero-match result to prove a fact absent. It does not search aliases, paraphrases or the whole corpus.\nINPUT: ref must be a received dN; text is a nonblank string <=2000 characters; optional integer start>=0; ignore_case is boolean, default false.\nOUTPUT: ok/document/snapshot/matches gives original start/end positions and exact excerpts, case_sensitive and next_start. Matches are positions_not_evidence, not citable eN.\nWORKFLOW: find -> read the matched range with enough preceding subject context -> compare evidence. Change literal terms or use goal-read rather than repeating zero matches.","name":"find","parameters":{"additionalProperties":false,"properties":{"ignore_case":{"type":"boolean"},"ref":{"pattern":"^d[1-9][0-9]*$","type":"string"},"start":{"minimum":0,"type":"integer"},"text":{"maxLength":2000,"minLength":1,"pattern":"\\S","type":"string"}},"required":["ref","text"],"type":"object"}},"type":"function"},{"function":{"description":"FUNCTION: Locate earlier delivered evidence, received search navigation and optional note history without external retrieval.\nUSE WHEN: A known lead or earlier raw window has left the active context; recover it instead of re-searching the same phrase.\nAVOID: Do not cite a recall excerpt as new evidence. It cannot search unreceived backend data; a lexical miss is not a proof of absence.\nINPUT: query is a nonblank string <=2000 characters. Use a discriminating entity or relation already encountered.\nOUTPUT: ok/matches has ref or note key, exact excerpt and kind. eN can be replayed; dN can be opened; inactive notes are historical model judgments, not evidence.\nWORKFLOW: recall -> read({\"ref\":\"eN\"}) for exact recovery, or read({\"ref\":\"dN\"}) for an unread page. Then update only the gap that changed.","name":"recall","parameters":{"additionalProperties":false,"properties":{"query":{"maxLength":2000,"minLength":1,"pattern":"\\S","type":"string"}},"required":["query"],"type":"object"}},"type":"function"},{"function":{"description":"FUNCTION: Maintain a small optional free-text scratchpad; not verified facts or a prerequisite to submit.\nUSE WHEN: A brief reusable alias, rejected route or independent reminder will help later. Prefer update_gap for the active research question when available.\nAVOID: Do not summarize every page, copy the full question, or repeatedly write the same note. Notes do not automatically retract dependent plans.\nINPUT: put requires op/key/text/anchors; text <=600 characters, anchors is <=6 delivered eN. delete requires op/key only. Keys are 1..32 ASCII letters/digits/_/-. Capacity is explicit.\nOUTPUT: ok/key/changed and kind=agent_note_not_evidence, or deleted. A no-op is not research progress. Errors do not silently alter saved notes.\nWORKFLOW: Optionally save a useful observation alongside another already-planned action; keep sources in the archive and finish without cleanup.","name":"notes","parameters":{"oneOf":[{"additionalProperties":false,"properties":{"anchors":{"items":{"pattern":"^e[1-9][0-9]*$","type":"string"},"maxItems":6,"minItems":0,"type":"array","uniqueItems":true},"key":{"pattern":"^[A-Za-z0-9_-]{1,32}$","type":"string"},"op":{"const":"put"},"text":{"maxLength":600,"minLength":1,"pattern":"\\S","type":"string"}},"required":["op","key","text","anchors"],"type":"object"},{"additionalProperties":false,"properties":{"key":{"pattern":"^[A-Za-z0-9_-]{1,32}$","type":"string"},"op":{"const":"delete"}},"required":["op","key"],"type":"object"}]}},"type":"function"},{"function":{"description":"FUNCTION: Explicitly submit the requested answer with source windows, or explicitly abstain.\nUSE WHEN: The requested core answer has sufficient support, or the available budget/evidence cannot resolve it. No note, gap update or audit is mandatory.\nAVOID: Do not claim every clue is verified when it is not. Do not change established into expanded, by-year into in-year, or hide unresolved numeric conflicts in a long explanation.\nINPUT: Answer branch requires answer and refs. Nonblank strings are <=8000 characters; string-integer-v1 additionally permits plain JSON integers, not bools/floats, rendered as decimal text. Strings stay exact. Abstention requires abstain=true and nonblank reason<=2000 characters. Never mix branches. refs contains only delivered eN, <=16; configured source and literal-format requirements still apply.\nOUTPUT: ok/terminal records submitted or abstained. Submitted answer is text with refs/basis and any representation metadata. Legal references are NOT semantic verification. Explicit prefix/suffix constraints still apply.\nWORKFLOW: Finish last in a response, based only on evidence already received for that decision. Never combine a new read and a guessed citation to its unseen result. In FINAL only finish is allowed.","name":"finish","parameters":{"oneOf":[{"additionalProperties":false,"properties":{"answer":{"maxLength":8000,"minLength":1,"pattern":"\\S","type":["string","integer"]},"refs":{"items":{"pattern":"^e[1-9][0-9]*$","type":"string"},"maxItems":16,"minItems":0,"type":"array","uniqueItems":true}},"required":["answer","refs"],"type":"object"},{"additionalProperties":false,"properties":{"abstain":{"const":true},"reason":{"maxLength":2000,"minLength":1,"pattern":"\\S","type":"string"}},"required":["abstain","reason"],"type":"object"}]}},"type":"function"},{"function":{"description":"FUNCTION: Replace the one active research question and retain a bounded history of explicit model judgments; it is NOT an automatic verifier.\nUSE WHEN: A candidate, unresolved relation, rejection or conflict changed, or repeated searches need a concrete next objective. Use sparingly, not after every tool.\nAVOID: Do not turn the original question or your prior guess into evidence. Updating a gap does not count as a new observation and does not reset a search loop.\nINPUT: subject/question/basis are nonblank <=400 characters; kind and status follow schema; evidence_refs are <=6 received eN, navigation_refs <=6 received dN; next_action is one named existing tool. supported/rejected/conflict require evidence_refs. Supply a concise decision basis, not a transcript.\nOUTPUT: ok/changed/current returns agent_judgment_not_verified with revision. At most four previous distinct gap versions remain in the active view; the full update is journaled. Sources are checked for delivery, not entailment.\nWORKFLOW: Observe -> identify one missing relationship or record a real rejection -> select read on an available page or search for a new relation. An update may share a response with known independent actions; no update is required before finish.","name":"update_gap","parameters":{"additionalProperties":false,"properties":{"basis":{"maxLength":400,"minLength":1,"pattern":"\\S","type":"string"},"evidence_refs":{"items":{"pattern":"^e[1-9][0-9]*$","type":"string"},"maxItems":6,"minItems":0,"type":"array","uniqueItems":true},"kind":{"enum":["locate","read","relation","disambiguate","conflict","recover"]},"navigation_refs":{"items":{"pattern":"^d[1-9][0-9]*$","type":"string"},"maxItems":6,"minItems":0,"type":"array","uniqueItems":true},"next_action":{"enum":["search","read","find","recall","finish"]},"question":{"maxLength":400,"minLength":1,"pattern":"\\S","type":"string"},"status":{"enum":["open","supported","rejected","conflict"]},"subject":{"maxLength":400,"minLength":1,"pattern":"\\S","type":"string"}},"required":["subject","question","kind","status","evidence_refs","navigation_refs","next_action","basis"],"type":"object"}},"type":"function"}]}